---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import ConnectsStrip from '../components/ConnectsStrip.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import Fig2ThreadDiagram from '../components/Fig2ThreadDiagram.astro';
import StepRow from '../components/StepRow.astro';
import ServiceList from '../components/ServiceList.astro';
import ContactSection from '../components/ContactSection.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Base>
  <Header />
  <Hero />
  <ConnectsStrip />

  <Section
    eyebrow="§ 01 · The problem"
    heading="Nothing is broken. That’s the problem."
    headingWidth={16}
    headingGap={24}
    noteLabel="Worth asking"
  >
    <p class="txt" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      Every tool you bought works. The accounting software does accounting. The payment
      processor takes payments. The scheduling app schedules. Each one is fine on its own,
      and none of them know the others exist.
    </p>
    <p class="txt" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      So a person becomes the connection between them. Someone reads a number off one
      screen and types it into another. Someone opens four tabs to answer one question.
      Someone remembers to send the invoice. That work never appears as a line item on
      anything, which is exactly why it never gets fixed.
    </p>
    <p class="txt">That work is what Thread removes.</p>

    <Fragment slot="note">
      What does a customer touch, from first contact to final payment? Which of those steps
      happens because a person <strong>remembers</strong> to do it? Most businesses can’t
      answer the first question from a single screen. That gap is the work.
    </Fragment>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="how"
    eyebrow="§ 02 · How it works"
    heading="One line through everything you run."
    headingWidth={18}
  >
    <p class="lead" style="max-width:56ch">
      Thread doesn’t replace the tools you already pay for. It builds the layer that runs
      through them, so information moves on its own and your team looks in one place.
    </p>

    <Figure caption="Fig. 2 · The thread, drawn">
      <Fig2ThreadDiagram />
    </Figure>

    <StepRow />
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="services"
    eyebrow="§ 03 · Services"
    heading="Four kinds of work, one underlying job."
    headingWidth={16}
    noteLabel="Engagement shape"
    docGap={44}
  >
    <p class="lead" style="max-width:52ch">
      Most projects need more than one of these. The map is how you find out which, and it
      will say so when the answer is none of them.
    </p>

    <Fragment slot="note">
      Fixed scope at a fixed price, or a monthly retainer for ongoing build and support. No
      hourly billing and no change-order games.
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="after">
      <ServiceList />

      <p class="txt" style="margin-top:34px;max-width:52ch">
        <strong>Care plan.</strong> Hosting, monitoring, small changes and a standing
        monthly hour, offered as a line item in every proposal. Software nobody maintains
        gets harder to change and easier to abandon.
      </p>
      <p class="txt" style="margin-top:16px;max-width:52ch">
        <strong>Rescue work.</strong> Already have something half-built? Thread also takes
        on software somebody else started: a tool that mostly works, an automation nobody
        understands, a system the person who built it no longer supports.
      </p>
    </Fragment>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="map"
    eyebrow="§ 04 · The systems map"
    heading="Start with a map, not a quote."
    headingWidth={14}
    noteLabel="Two per month"
  >
    <p class="lead" style="max-width:56ch">
      Before anyone talks about building anything, Thread draws how your work actually
      moves today. One workflow, start to finish. Every tool it touches, every handoff,
      every point where a person is doing a job a system should be doing.
    </p>
    <p class="txt" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      You get the map, a list of where the time is going, and a ranked view of what’s worth
      fixing first. It’s free, it’s yours to keep, and there’s no obligation to do anything
      with it. If the honest answer is that you don’t need custom software, the map will
      say so.
    </p>
    <p class="txt" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      <strong>You fill in a short intake.</strong> Ten minutes. What the business does, what
      the process is, which tools it touches, what already went wrong.
    </p>
    <p class="txt" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      <strong>One conversation, ninety minutes.</strong> Thread walks the process with
      whoever actually does it, not just whoever owns it.
    </p>
    <p class="txt">
      <strong>You get the map within five business days.</strong> A drawn current-state
      diagram, an inventory of where time and information leak, and a ranked list of fixes
      with rough cost and effort against each one.
    </p>

    <div class="btn-row">
      <a href="#contact" class="btn">Start a systems map</a>
    </div>

    <Fragment slot="note">
      A map is real work, and it’s free, so it’s capped at two a month and one workflow
      each. Choose the process that costs you the most time: lead to invoice, job to
      payment, intake to delivery. Mapping a whole business is a paid engagement, and the
      free one is a reasonable way to find out whether it’s worth doing.
    </Fragment>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="work"
    eyebrow="§ 05 · Work"
    heading="Every project ships with its plate."
    headingWidth={18}
  >
    <p class="lead" style="max-width:56ch">
      Thread documents what it built and why, in plain English, so the knowledge lives with
      you and not only with the person who wrote it.
    </p>

    <p class="txt" style="margin-top:16px;max-width:56ch">
      Thread is a new practice. The work behind it isn’t. Selected projects from before the
      name existed will appear here, shown without client names, as permission comes in.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section
    id="about"
    eyebrow="§ 06 · About"
    heading="Built in Hawaiʻi. Useful anywhere."
    headingWidth={17}
    noteLabel="Working with a small practice"
  >
    <p class="txt" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      I’m Shauna, and Thread is my custom software practice, based in Honolulu. It takes on
      work across the islands, and on the mainland for people who would rather hire someone
      who answers the phone than a firm that assigns them an account manager.
    </p>
    <p class="txt" style="margin-bottom:16px">
      Being here shapes the work. Island businesses tend to run lean, on tools chosen years
      ago for reasons that were good at the time, with no appetite for tearing everything
      out and starting over. That constraint turns out to be the right way to build
      software for anyone.
    </p>
    <p class="txt">
      The name is the whole idea. A thread runs through everything a business already uses
      and turns a pile of separate tools into one thing you can operate.
    </p>

    <Fragment slot="note">
      Thread is me. Everything runs in your accounts, on your infrastructure. You own the
      source outright, and every build ships documented well enough that another developer
      could pick it up without calling me.
    </Fragment>
  </Section>

  <ContactSection />
  <Footer />
</Base>
